  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nSunday will be a down day for shower activity with a brief period of \nshortwave ridging and some slight dry air advection from the \nnorthwest. This will lead to considerably less clouds, virtually no \nrain chances and temperatures climbing back into the upper 70s by \nMonday. Models remain in good agreement that another northern \nPacific waves will continue to be ejected southeastward toward the \nU.S. West Coast starting early next week. Currently models suggest \nthe next potential system reaching the California coast early Monday \nbefore reaching our region Monday night into Tuesday, where \nadditional rain chances will return to the forecast with NBM PoPs \npeaking between 50-80% in south-central AZ and the higher terrain \nareas, however this far out this may need to be adjusted based of \nhow the track of this low progresses. By Wednesday models are \npointing towards yet again another low system, however larger \ndiscrepancies between models are evident for this system. Due to \nthis confidence is low for rain chances by mid to late next week as \nthings are too inconsistent at this time. \n\nDespite if these next systems bring rain to the region or not, a \nmore significant cooling trend can be counted on by the middle of \nthe week as these series of weather systems move in from the \nnorthwest. They will likely usher in a cooler air mass with \nafternoon highs trending more towards the upper 60s to low 70s, the \nhigher or lower end of this range largely being dependent on the \ndepth and position of the systems.",
